<h2>What third-party funding is</h2>
<p>Third-party funding (TPF) is an arrangement by which a person with no pre-existing interest in a dispute finances a party's costs of pursuing it — court fees, arbitral fees, counsel fees, experts — usually on a non-recourse basis. If the claim succeeds, the funder receives an agreed return, commonly a multiple of the amount advanced or a percentage of the recovery. If the claim fails, the funder bears the loss. The model is distinct from a loan: the funder's return depends on the outcome, and the funded party ordinarily repays nothing on defeat.</p>

<h2>The legal foundation in India</h2>
<p>English notions of maintenance and champerty — under which funding another's litigation was itself unlawful — were never adopted as rigid rules in India. The position that has long prevailed is that a funding agreement by a <em>non-lawyer</em> is not per se void; it is simply a contract, enforceable unless it offends ordinary contract-law limits.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Contract Act limits</h4><p>An agreement that is extortionate, unconscionable, made for an improper object or contrary to public policy can be refused enforcement under Sections 23 and 28 principles. A funder's return so large that it swallows the claimant's recovery invites this scrutiny.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>The advocate's bar</h4><p>The Bar Council of India Rules prohibit advocates from charging fees contingent on results, sharing the fruits of litigation, or buying an interest in the actionable claim they are conducting. TPF in India is therefore a market for funders, not for counsel.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Procedural recognition</h4><p>State amendments to Order XXV of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908 in certain States empower courts to deal with a financier of the plaintiff's suit, including requiring security for the defendant's costs — an implicit recognition that funded litigation exists and is not forbidden.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Where funding is most used</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Commercial arbitration:</strong> high-value construction, infrastructure and shareholder disputes, where tribunal and institutional fees are heavy and recovery timelines long.</li>
    <li><strong>Insolvency estates:</strong> resolution professionals and liquidators pursuing avoidance claims or recoveries for the estate often lack funds; financing recoveries benefits the creditor body as a whole.</li>
    <li><strong>Group claims:</strong> homebuyers or investors with modest individual stakes but a strong collective claim.</li>
    <li><strong>Enforcement proceedings:</strong> funding the execution of a decree or award against a recalcitrant judgment-debtor, where the asset trail is costly to follow.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>The issues a funded party must think through</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Issue</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Control of the case</td><td>Settlement and strategy decisions should remain with the party and its counsel. A funder who controls the litigation invites public-policy objections and professional-conduct complications for counsel.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Privilege and confidentiality</td><td>Case assessments shared with a funder should be protected by carefully drafted confidentiality terms; share no more than the funder genuinely needs.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Adverse costs</td><td>If the claim fails, the opposite party's costs may be awarded. The agreement should say whether the funder bears them; in funded suits, courts in some States can direct the financier to give security.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Arbitrator conflicts</td><td>An arbitrator connected to the funder can face disclosure and challenge questions. Tribunals may ask about funding for exactly this reason.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>The funder's return</td><td>Returns are commonly staged by time or recovery size. A return that leaves the claimant with a token share of its own claim risks being branded unconscionable.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Termination</td><td>A funder exiting mid-proceeding can strand the claim; termination should be restricted and transition funding addressed.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The regulatory horizon</h2>
<p>India has no dedicated TPF legislation, no licensing regime for funders, and no mandatory disclosure rule of general application. That leaves the field governed by contract law, professional-conduct rules for advocates, and the case-by-case supervision of courts and tribunals. Parties should expect tribunals — especially institutional ones — to ask about funding where it bears on conflicts or security for costs, and should expect enforcement courts to look at the fairness of the bargain if it is ever put in issue.</p>
<div class="note">
  <p>This article is general information on an evolving commercial-law topic, not legal advice. A funding agreement should be settled with independent professional advice on its specific terms.</p>
</div>
